            0.379,
            0.081
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 6"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.12,
            0.885,
            0.188
          ],
          "content": "What is special about safe pairs? They don’t have any common factor other than 1. Two numbers are said to be **co-prime** to each other if they have no common factor other than 1."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.196,
            0.885,
            0.241
          ],
          "content": "**Example:** As 15 and 39 have 3 as a common factor, they are not co-prime. But 4 and 9 are co-prime."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.165,
            0.248,
            0.788,
            0.32
          ],
          "content": "嶽 Which of the following pairs of numbers are co-prime? a. 18 and 35 b. 15 and 37 c. 30 and 415 d. 17 and 69 e. 81 and 18"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.331,
            0.814,
            0.379
          ],
          "content": "While playing the ‘idli-vada’ game with different number pairs, Anshu observed something interesting!"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.828,
            0.336,
            0.916,
            0.395
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_9_image_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.2,
            0.384,
            0.813,
            0.428
          ],
          "content": "1. Sometimes the first common multiple was the same as the product of the two numbers."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.2,
            0.429,
            0.813,
            0.474
          ],
          "content": "2. At other times the first common multiple was less than the product of the two numbers."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.475,
            0.813,
            0.521
          ],
          "content": "Find examples for each of the above. How is it related to the number pair being co-prime?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.53,
            0.318,
            0.552
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Co-prime art"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.556,
            0.815,
            0.672
          ],
          "content": "Observe the following thread art. The first diagram has 12 pegs and the thread is tied to every fourth peg (we say that the thread-gap is 4). The second diagram has 13 pegs and the thread-gap is 3. What about the other diagrams? Observe these pictures, share and discuss your findings in class."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
